window.PREVIEW_DEEP = Object.assign(window.PREVIEW_DEEP||{}, {"jemas-2025": [{"q": "Which of the following is a mature defence mechanism?", "concept": "The correct answer is altruism. George Vaillant arranged the ego defence mechanisms into a developmental hierarchy running from pathological, through immature and neurotic, up to mature. The MATURE defences are the healthiest and most adaptive: altruism, sublimation, humour, suppression, and anticipation. Altruism is the constructive channelling of one's own needs into vicarious but genuinely helpful service to others, which is why it counts as mature rather than merely a reaction against a wish. Of the four options here only altruism belongs to that mature list. Repression and intellectualisation are neurotic-level defences and rationalisation is likewise a neurotic defence, all lower on Vaillant's ladder. So the one mature defence among the choices is altruism.", "traps": {"b": "Repression, the automatic pushing of unacceptable thoughts out of awareness, sits in Vaillant's neurotic tier, not the mature one.", "c": "Intellectualisation (dealing with a conflict in abstract, emotionless, theoretical terms) is a neurotic defence, so it is not the mature option.", "d": "Rationalisation (offering self-justifying but false reasons for one's behaviour) is a neurotic defence, not a mature one."}}, {"q": "According to Alfred Adler's individual psychology, human behaviour is best characterised as:", "concept": "The correct answer is that behaviour is purposeful and goal-directed. This is the heart of Adler's teleology: he held that people are pulled by future goals (the striving for superiority or mastery) rather than merely pushed by past causes. Key Adlerian ideas all follow from this: fictional finalism (we act as if guided by an imagined future ideal), the striving for superiority born out of compensation for felt inferiority, and the person's unique style of life. Option (d), development through psychosocial stages, is Erikson; option (b) is Freud's instinct model; option (c) is behaviourism. Only (a) captures Adler's core view that human behaviour is goal-directed and purposeful.", "traps": {"b": "Instinct-driven, libido-based determinism is Freud's model, not Adler's; Adler broke with Freud precisely to stress purpose over drive.", "c": "Stimulus-response conditioning is the behaviourist account (Watson, Skinner), not Adlerian teleology.", "d": "Development through eight psychosocial stages is Erikson's theory, not Adler's."}}, {"q": "The biologically based, inborn and relatively stable style of emotional and behavioural reactivity is termed:", "concept": "The correct answer is temperament. Temperament is the biologically based, constitutional, and relatively stable foundation of an individual's characteristic emotional and behavioural reactivity and self-regulation, observable from early infancy (as in the Thomas and Chess easy/difficult/slow-to-warm-up typology). Character, by contrast, is the learned and value-laden part of personality acquired through socialisation, and personality is the whole integrated system. Disposition is too loose and general to be the technical term. Because the question specifies the inborn, biologically rooted reactive style, the precise answer is temperament.", "traps": {"a": "Disposition is a broad, everyday term for a person's general tendency; it is not the specific construct for the inborn biological reactive style.", "b": "Character refers to the learned, valued, and moral aspects of personality shaped by socialisation, not the biologically given baseline.", "c": "Personality is the whole organised pattern of thinking, feeling and behaving, far broader than the innate reactive core the item describes."}}, {"q": "In personality psychology, a trait is best defined as:", "concept": "The correct answer is that a trait is a specific attribute along which people differ consistently and stably. In the trait tradition (Allport, Cattell, Eysenck, and the modern Big Five), a trait is an enduring dimension of individual difference: a relatively stable, consistent way of behaving, thinking, and feeling that a person carries across situations and time, and on which individuals can be placed and compared. The defining features are consistency (across situations) and stability (across time), which is exactly what option (b) states. This distinguishes a trait from a fleeting state (option a), from the moral 'character' component (option c), and from an unconscious drive conflict (option d).", "traps": {"a": "A transient, situation-bound feeling is a STATE, the conceptual opposite of a trait; traits are defined by their cross-situational consistency and stability over time.", "c": "The moral and ethical component of the self is closer to 'character' or the superego, not the definition of a trait.", "d": "An unconscious conflict between drives is a psychodynamic notion, not the trait-theory definition."}}, {"q": "The satiety centre, whose activation signals the organism to stop eating, is located in the:", "concept": "The correct answer is the ventromedial hypothalamus (VMH). Classic dual-centre lesion and stimulation studies established two opposing hypothalamic feeding centres. The ventromedial hypothalamus is the SATIETY centre: stimulating it inhibits eating, and destroying it produces hyperphagia and obesity (the VMH syndrome). The lateral hypothalamus is the HUNGER or feeding centre: stimulating it drives eating, and lesioning it causes aphagia and starvation. The mnemonic Sihan can lock in is 'Ventromedial = Very Much Full (satiety); Lateral = Lacking food (hunger).' So the satiety centre is the ventromedial hypothalamus.", "traps": {"a": "The lateral hypothalamus is the HUNGER (feeding) centre; its stimulation starts eating and its destruction causes aphagia, the exact opposite of satiety.", "c": "The anterior hypothalamus is chiefly involved in thermoregulation (heat loss) and parasympathetic control, not the satiety signal.", "d": "The posterior hypothalamus governs heat conservation and sympathetic arousal, not the stop-eating satiety signal."}}, {"q": "A value or principle is described as universal when it:", "concept": "The correct answer is that a value or principle is universal when it holds for all cases, at all times, and in all places. Universality is fundamentally a claim about scope: a universal proposition admits no exceptions across instances, across time, and across place or culture. Something confined to a single culture or era (option b) is by definition particular or relative, not universal; something true only under special conditions (option c) or only after repeated verification (option d) is context-bound or provisional. What makes a value universal is precisely that it applies to all, always, and everywhere, which is option (a).", "traps": {"b": "Being bound to one culture or era makes something relative or particular, which is the direct opposite of universal.", "c": "Holding only under controlled conditions describes a context-limited empirical finding, not a universal principle.", "d": "Requiring repeated empirical verification describes an inductive, provisional truth; universality is a matter of scope (all cases, times, places), not of how it is tested."}}]});
